NAME
    sh - the Ouroboros shell

SYNOPSIS
    sh    (the boot shell; started automatically, not run by hand)

DESCRIPTION
    The default interactive shell - a real userland program (EL0), loaded from
    disk at boot, that reads a command line, runs it, and prompts again with
    "$ ". It is "just a program": the same relocatable binary any /bin command
    is, given the keyboard because it is task 0.

    A command is looked up in this order: first the shell's own BUILTINS
    (below), then a program on $PATH (default /bin) by bare name - so typing
    `ls` runs /bin/LS. A /bin command runs as a separate task, spawned with the
    arguments and the shell's current directory, then reaped; its exit status is
    shown by `ps`. Unknown names report "unknown command".

BUILTINS
    The commands the shell runs itself, because each touches shell-only state,
    job control, an unmounted disk, or machine power - things a spawned program
    cannot do. Everything else lives in /bin (run `ls /bin`).

    Shell state
        cd [path]        change the working directory (default /)
        bind ...         graft one path onto another in this task's namespace
        env              list environment variables
        set NAME=VALUE   set a variable (also: export); $NAME expands it
        unset NAME       remove a variable
        exit             end the shell (refused for the boot shell)

    Job control
        ps               list tasks, their names, and exit codes
        kill <n>         destroy task <n>
        fg <n>           hand the keyboard to a task (reverts on its death, ^C)
        wait <n>         block until task <n> exits
        exec <prog> ...  spawn a program explicitly (same as typing its name)

    Disk management (run with nothing mounted)
        mount            list mounts; mount -a mount the disk; -r host:port,
                         -p, -c, -n bind a remote/proc/console/net resource
        unmount          drop the current mount
        erase            zero a disk's leading sectors
        partition [fs]   write a single-partition MBR (fat32|exfat|ext2)
        format [fs]      lay a filesystem into the partition (mkfs)

    Cluster
        cpu host:port <command>   run <command> on another machine, reading
                                  this machine's files at /host (Plan 9 cpu)

    Power
        shutdown / poweroff       power the machine off (PSCI)
        halt                      mask interrupts and stop

    help                 list the builtins

SYNTAX
    $VAR              expand a variable (set with `set`); $PATH drives lookup
    cmd > file        redirect output to a file (truncate)
    cmd >> file       redirect output, appending
    a | b | c         pipe: each stage a program, one builtin allowed anywhere
    a | b > file      pipe then redirect the last stage
    *  ?              filename wildcards, expanded against the filesystem
    Tab               complete the last word as a filename
    <command> -?      one-line usage for any command, builtin or /bin
    man <command>     the full manual page (this is `man sh`)

    Interactive commands that read the keyboard (the pager, `more`/`less`) run
    as /bin programs because the foreground task owns the keyboard; a pipeline's
    last stage is given the keyboard, so `ls | more` pages correctly.

SEE ALSO
    help, man, ls, mount, cpu, ps
